1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,050
Listen to a talk in a biology class.

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:09,420
In the early 1900s, sea otters

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,586
marine mammals that feed on invertebrates including sea urchins,

4
00:00:11,586 --> 00:00:17,250
were nearly wiped out along the United States Pacific Coast by fur hunters.

5
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:23,000
With otters gone, urchin numbers exploded, destroying kelp forests.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,130
That kelp is a type of seaweed and it's sea urchins favorite food.

7
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:33,160
This left countless marine species without the protective habitat that kelp forest provided to them.

8
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:37,120
In 1972, hunting sea otters was banned.

9
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,660
As otters made a comeback, urchin numbers dropped

10
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,170
kelp forest grew back, and biodiversity flourished.

11
00:00:44,170 --> 00:00:47,515
Scientists realized otters were a keystone species,

12
00:00:47,515 --> 00:00:53,250
organisms whose impact on ecosystems is far greater than their numbers suggest.

13
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:57,280
A similar case occurred in Yellowstone National Park

14
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:01,020
Wolves, the park's top predator, had been eradicated.

15
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:05,755
Populations of elk, a deer-like animal and a favorite prey for wolves,

16
00:01:05,755 --> 00:01:08,911
soared and their huge populations damaged local vegetation,

17
00:01:08,911 --> 00:01:14,040
including the Willow and Aspen trees for which the park had been famous.

18
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:19,220
In the 1990s, wolves were reintroduced to the park to get the elk population under control.

19
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:24,080
But the wolves didn't just cut elk numbers, they changed elk behavior.

20
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:26,340
Afraid of being eaten by wolves

21
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:30,000
the elk avoided stream side areas where they're especially vulnerable.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,940
This helped Willow and Aspen trees reestablish themselves there

23
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:35,432
and the trees brought back Beavers,

24
00:01:35,432 --> 00:01:40,140
animals that live in streams and build dams to create ponds for themselves.

25
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:43,952
The Beaver dams created habitats for fish,

26
00:01:43,952 --> 00:01:47,160
birds and amphibians, a huge cascading effect

27
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:51,030
because of this other keystone species.
